{{Short description|Region in the Horn of Africa}}
{{for|the concept of reincartion in Serer religion|Ciiɗ (Serer religion)}}
{{Original research|date=February 2022}}

'''Ciid''' or '''<nowiki>'</nowiki>Iid''' ({{langx|so|Arlo Ciideed}}) is an archaic native geographic name for the land between the region of Mudug and the Nugaal Valley, roughly congruous with the northern [[Bookh]]. As such, Ciid constitutes the tripoint of the two former colonial powers [[British Empire|Britain]] and [[Italy]], as well as neighboring Ethiopia, thus situating [[Mudug]] immediately southeast of Ciid, the [[Nugaal Valley]] immediately north of Ciid, and [[Haud]] to the west of Ciid. One historian referred to it as the syrup-colored land and it is today embodied by Ciid towns such as Futoxum, [[Magacley]], [[Qoriley]], [[Biriqodey]], [[Beerdhiga]] and [[Gumburka Cagaare]].<ref>Diiwaanka gabayadii, 1856-1921 - Maxamad Cabdulle Xasan, 1999, page 41</ref><ref>Suugaanta Nabadda Iyo Colaadda, 
Rashiid Sheekh Cabdillaahi Gadhweyne, Axmed Aw Geeddi, Ismaciil Aw Aadan · 2009</ref>

Ciid constitutes the northernmost parts of the disputed Somali-Ethiopian territory outlined in the 16 May 1908 Italo-Ethiopian border agreement also called the 1908 Convention. A 2001 Journal from [[Indiana University]] describes Ciid as partially overlapping with [[Boocame District]] by referring to Ciid as ''north of the Mudug region and the west of the Garowe region''.<ref>Journal of the Anglo-Somali Society - Issues 30-33, 2001 - PAGE 18</ref>

{{Short description|Field of technology}}

{{About|[[technology]] with properties essential to life|life engineered in biotechnology and [[synthetic biology]]|Synthetic life}}
{{for multi|biomimetic technology|Biomimetics|biologically inspired engineering|Bionics}} 

'''Living technology''' is the field of [[technology]] that derives its functionality and usefulness from the properties that make natural organisms alive (see [[life]]).  It may be seen as a technological subfield of both [[artificial life]] and [[complex systems]] and is relevant beyond [[biotechnology]] to [[nanotechnology]], [[information technology]], [[artificial intelligence]], [[environmental technology]] and socioeconomic technology for managing [[human society]].

{{Short description|None}}
{{Prose|date=May 2021}}
'''Visual impairment in art''' is a limited topic covered by research, with its focus being on how [[visually impaired]] people are represented in artwork throughout history. This is commonly portrayed through the inclusion of objects such as canes and dogs to symbolize blindness,<ref name=":0" /> which is the most frequently depicted visual impairment in art. Many notable figures in art history, such as [[Leonardo da Vinci]], [[Claude Monet]], and [[Georgia O'Keeffe]], were visually impaired, or theorized to be so.

