[[Human cannibalism|Cannibalism]] is depicted in literary and other imaginative works across history.<ref name=":0">{{Cite book |last=Blurton |first=Heather |title=Cannibalism in high medieval English literature |date=2007 |publisher=Palgrave Macmillan |isbn=978-1-4039-7443-3 |location=Basingstoke |oclc=123798600}}</ref><ref name=":1">{{Cite news |last=Rawson |first=Claude |date=24 January 1985 |title=Eating people |language=en |volume=07 |work=London Review of Books |issue=1 |url=https://www.lrb.co.uk/the-paper/v07/n01/claude-rawson/eating-people |access-date=8 April 2022 |issn=0260-9592}}</ref> Homer's ''[[Odyssey]]'', ''[[Beowulf]]'', Shakespeare's ''[[Titus Andronicus]]'', Flaubert's ''[[Salammbô|Salammbo]]'', and Melville's ''[[Moby-Dick|Moby Dick]]'' are prominent examples.<ref name=":1" />

